# Indian Music and Painting: Two Systems, Many Schools

> North and south India hear classical music differently — two
> systems, one theory of raga. Its walls wear painting differently
> too — Mughal miniatures beside Rajasthani courts, folk walls
> beside gold-leaf temple panels. The GK paper samples both worlds:
> a gharana here, a Bani Thani there, an instrument matched to its
> maestro. This lesson tunes both strings.

## 1. The Two Classical Systems

[definition]
India has **two systems of classical music**: **Hindustani** (North
Indian), shaped in the medieval courts with Persian and Central
Asian influence, and **Carnatic** (South Indian), which retained
and systematised the older, devotional Sangita tradition. Both are
built on **raga** (melodic framework) and **tala** (rhythmic
cycle); they differ in form, instruments, and repertoire.
[/definition]

| Axis | Hindustani | Carnatic |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Region | North, west, east, central India | Andhra, Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil Nadu |
| Major forms | Dhrupad, khayal, thumri, tarana | Kriti, varnam, ragam-tanam-pallavi |
| Composition emphasis | Improvised elaboration (alap, jod, jhala) | Composed kritis of the trinity |
| Main percussion | Tabla | Mridangam |
| Typical instruments | Sitar, sarod, shehnai, sarangi, santoor | Veena, violin, nadaswaram, mridangam |
| Foundational text | Contemporary gharana lineage | Sangita Ratnakara and the trinity's corpus |

The **gharana** system is Hindustani music's signature institution —
a lineage of master-discelle training with a recognisable style:
**Gwalior** (the oldest khayal gharana), **Agra**, **Jaipur-Atrauli**,
**Kirana**, **Patiala**, **Rampur-Sahaswan**, and the instrumental
**Imdadkhani (Etawah)** school of sitar. Questions ask "which
gharana is the oldest" (Gwalior) or match a maestro to a gharana.

The **Carnatic trinity** — **Tyagaraja, Muthuswami Dikshitar, and
Syama Sastri** (all born in Tiruvarur, late 18th century) —
composed the kriti repertoire that anchors the system. Tyagaraja's
*Pancharatna kritis* remain the exam's most cited composition set.

## 2. The Maestro and Instrument Ladder

| Instrument | Maestro | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sitar | Ravi Shankar | Took Indian music to the world stage (Woodstock, the Beatles) |
| Sarod | Amjad Ali Khan | Bangash lineage of the Senia gharana |
| Shehnai | Bismillah Khan | Associated with the Kashi Vishwanath temple; Red Fort on Independence Day |
| Santoor | Shivkumar Sharma | Brought the Kashmiri folk instrument to the classical stage |
| Flute (bansuri) | Hariprasad Chaurasia | — |
| Tabla | Zakir Hussain, Alla Rakha | The father-son standard |
| Sarangi | Ram Narayan | Elevated it from accompaniment to solo |
| Violin (Carnatic & fusion) | L. Subramaniam, Kunnakudi Vaidyanathan | Adapted to Carnatic music in the 19th century |
| Veena | Emani Sankara Sastry, Jayanthi Kumaresh | Saraswati's instrument |
| Mridangam | Palghat Mani Iyer, T.K. Murthy | The Carnatic pulse |
| Nadaswaram | T.N. Rajaratnam Pillai, Sheik Chinna Moulana | The temple/auspicious-occasion oboe |
| Hindustani vocals | Bhimsen Joshi, Pandit Jasraj, Kishori Amonkar | — |

Bharat Ratna laureates from music — **M.S. Subbulakshmi (1998, the
first musician so honoured), Lata Mangeshkar (2001), Bismillah Khan
(2008), Bhimsen Joshi (2008)** — form a closed set the exam quotes
whenever it mixes awards with arts.

## 3. The Painting Schools — Court, Wall, and Folk

| School | Region / era | Signature |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mughal miniatures | Akbar to Shah Jahan | Naturalistic portraits, battle and court scenes; masters Daswant and Basawan under Akbar |
| Rajput / Rajasthani | Mewar, Bundi, Kishangarh, Jaipur... | Bold colour, Krishna-Radha themes; Kishangarh's **Bani Thani** is the "Mona Lisa of India" |
| Pahari | Himalayan foothills — Basohli, Kangra, Guler | Kangra's lyrical Gita-Govinda series; Basohli's vivid primary colours |
| Deccani | Golconda, Bijapur, Ahmednagar | Distinct sultanate style, rich palette |
| Company style | 18th–19th c. | Indian artists painting for British patrons, hybrid naturalism |
| Bengal School | Early 20th c. | Abanindranath Tagore's nationalist revival (*Bharat Mata*) |
| Progressive Artists' Group | Bombay, 1947 | F.N. Souza, M.F. Husain, S.H. Raza, Tyeb Mehta — modernism after independence |

[definition]
**Bani Thani**, the Kishangarh masterpiece of Raja Sawant Singh's
reign, is often called the **"Mona Lisa of India"** — an elongated,
arched-eyebrow portrait of the poet-king's beloved, embodying the
Kishangarh face that defines the school.
[/definition]

## 4. The Folk Walls and the Modern Masters

| Folk tradition | Region | Look |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Madhubani (Mithila) | Bihar | Fish, peacocks, fertility motifs in natural pigments, by women artists |
| Warli | Maharashtra (tribal) | Stick figures in white rice-paste on mud walls; the tarpa dance spiral |
| Pattachitra | Odisha and Bengal | Cloth-scroll narrative of temple deities (Odisha) and scroll singers (Bengal) |
| Phad | Rajasthan | Scroll paintings of Pabuji and Devnarayan epics, carried by Bhopa singers |
| Kalighat pat | 19th-c. Calcutta | Fast watercolour satire sold near the Kali temple |
| Tanjore (Thanjavur) | Tamil Nadu | Gilded and gem-set panels of deities on wood |
| Picchwai | Nathdwara, Rajasthan | Cloth hangings for the Shrinathji shrine |
| Kalamkari | Andhra Pradesh | Hand-painted/ block-printed textile narrative art |

The modern trio the exam cannot skip: **Raja Ravi Varma** — the
Kerala prince who fused European oil realism with Indian mythology
and is called the **father of modern Indian painting**; **Amrita
Sher-Gil**, the Indo-Hungarian pioneer of Indian modernism; and
**M.F. Husain**, the most famous (and most controversial) figure of
the Progressive era. Behind them stands the ancient wall: the
**Ajanta murals** (c. 2nd century BCE–6th century CE), Buddhist
cave paintings whose graceful lines every school later borrowed
from.

[example]
The crossover trap: "Bani Thani is a celebrated painting of which
school?" — options usually include Kangra (Pahari) and Mughal as
decoys. The answer is Kishangarh, the Rajasthani school. Its
inverse: "The Gita Govinda series belongs to which Pahari school?"
— Kangra. And the globalised version: "'Mona Lisa of India' refers
to —" Bani Thani again. Paintings, like trophies, travel in pairs
of school and signature work.
[/example]

[summary]
Music: two systems — Hindustani (dhrupad, khayal, thumri; gharanas
like Gwalior, Jaipur, Kirana; tabla, sitar, sarod, shehnai,
santoor) and Carnatic (the trinity Tyagaraja, Dikshitar, Syama
Sastri; kriti repertoire; mridangam, veena, violin, nadaswaram).
Match maestros to instruments: Ravi Shankar (sitar), Amjad Ali Khan
(sarod), Bismillah Khan (shehnai), Shivkumar Sharma (santoor),
Chaurasia (flute), Zakir Hussain (tabla). Bharat Ratna musicians:
Subbulakshmi, Lata, Bismillah Khan, Bhimsen Joshi. Painting: Mughal
miniatures; Rajasthani schools with Kishangarh's Bani Thani;
Pahari (Kangra's Gita Govinda); folk walls — Madhubani, Warli,
Pattachitra, Phad, Kalighat, Tanjore; moderns — Raja Ravi Varma
(father of modern Indian painting), Amrita Sher-Gil, M.F. Husain;
and behind everything, Ajanta.
[/summary]
